  "rule": "Find the largest connected single-colour object in the noisy input and output a 3x3 grid filled with its colour; total colour frequency is irrelevant.",
  "scope": "Supported: grids 15..25 per side (originals 20x20), background 0, 3..5 solid blobs (rectangles 3..7 per side with optional chipped corners) in distinct colours, separated by at least one cell, 2..3 noise colours disjoint from blob colours scattered at density 0.3..0.5. Not supported: non-zero backgrounds, blobs of identical colours, multi-colour blobs, hole-containing blobs, noise that could form clusters rivalling the blobs.",
  "constraints": [
    "Largest blob area is unique, exceeding the second largest by at least 2 cells.",
    "Blob colours are pairwise distinct and disjoint from noise colours, so same-colour merging is impossible.",
    "Blob bounding boxes are separated by at least one empty cell in both directions.",
    "Every noise cluster, measured with 8-connectivity, has at most win_size - 2 cells, so it cannot win under 4- or 8-connected labelling.",
    "Colour 0 is strictly the most frequent colour so background detection is unambiguous.",
    "Noise colours typically outnumber the winning blob in total cells, keeping the frequency-count distractor alive."
